    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3258.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 706,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it is so important.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several unique and unusual properties that make it unlike almost any other substance on Earth:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals). This is why it can transport vital nutrients through living things and carve through rock over time.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats on water, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, allowing marine life to survive the winter.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nEarth is often called the \"Blue Planet\" because water covers about **71% of its surface**. \n* **Oceans:** About **96.5%** of Earth's water is stored in the oceans as saltwater.\n* **Freshwater:** Only about **2.5%** of Earth's water is fresh, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and soil.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Earth’s water is constantly recycled through the **hydrologic cycle**. Water evaporates from the oceans and lakes into the air (vapor), condenses into clouds, falls back to the ground as rain or snow (precipitation), and flows back into bodies of water. The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n---\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nAs far as science knows, **life cannot exist without water.** \n* **Inside Living Things:** The human body is made of about 60% water. Water fills our cells, cushions our joints, regulates our body temperature, and flushes out waste.\n* **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of species, from microscopic plankton to giant blue whales.\n* **Agriculture and Civilization:** Human history is defined by water. Early civilizations (like ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia) arose along major rivers. Today, we rely on water to grow crops, generate electricity (hydropower), manufacture goods, and sustain our cities.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is the lifeblood of our planet. It is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that make life, weather, and the very geography of Earth possible."
